From: Menno van Leeuwen <menno@vleeuwen.me>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: recover stopping discovery on inquiry complete

Some controllers can reject Inquiry Cancel while the host still has the
HCI_INQUIRY flag set.  If Inquiry Complete arrives afterward, the handler
clears HCI_INQUIRY but ignores it because discovery is no longer in
DISCOVERY_FINDING.  The management state then remains DISCOVERY_STOPPING
even though inquiry ended.

Treat Inquiry Complete as the final stop event while discovery is stopping.
This mirrors the existing remote-name-complete path, retains an active LE
scan, and emits the normal management discovering-off notification.  Warn
when this recovery path fires so tests can distinguish it from a clean
cancel.

Signed-off-by: Menno van Leeuwen <menno@vleeuwen.me>
---
 net/bluetooth/hci_event.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
@@ -2981,6 +2981,18 @@ static void hci_inquiry_complete_evt(struct hci_dev *hdev, void *data,
 
 	hci_dev_lock(hdev);
 
+	/* A rejected Inquiry Cancel can leave management in STOPPING while a
+	 * later Inquiry Complete proves that BR/EDR inquiry has ended.  Complete
+	 * that state transition without interrupting an active LE scan.
+	 */
+	if (discov->state == DISCOVERY_STOPPING &&
+	    (!hci_dev_test_flag(hdev, HCI_LE_SCAN) ||
+	     hdev->le_scan_type != LE_SCAN_ACTIVE)) {
+		bt_dev_warn(hdev, "recovering discovery stop on Inquiry Complete");
+		hci_discovery_set_state(hdev, DISCOVERY_STOPPED);
+		goto unlock;
+	}
+
 	if (discov->state != DISCOVERY_FINDING)
 		goto unlock;
 
-- 
